  user_input {
    user_response: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Final Aim Boundary Configuration & Progressive Steering Refinement\n\nYou are working on STARFALL, a browser-based 3D arcade space combat game.\n\nImplement the final mouse aiming boundary configuration and refine its integration with the existing progressive mouse steering system.\n\nThis is a focused gameplay control task. Preserve the existing working systems.\n\n## 1. Confirmed Design Decisions\n\nImplement these exact settings:\n\n- Aim boundary width: 45% of viewport width.\n- Aim boundary height: 45% of viewport height.\n- Boundary shape: Rounded rectangle.\n- Boundary position: Centered on the game viewport.\n- Steering transition: Smooth progressive acceleration.\n\nThe boundary dimensions refer to the full game viewport, not the browser window or the physical display.\n\nCalculate the boundary using the current game viewport dimensions so it remains correct when the browser is resized.\n\nFor a viewport of width W and height H:\n\n- Horizontal boundary half-extent: 0.225 \303\227 W.\n- Vertical boundary half-extent: 0.225 \303\227 H.\n\nThe virtual reticle should remain inside this rounded-rectangle precision region.\n\n## 2. Rounded Rectangle Boundary\n\nImplement a mathematically consistent rounded-rectangle boundary.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Center the boundary on the viewport.\n- Width = 45% of viewport width.\n- Height = 45% of viewport height.\n- Use configurable corner radius.\n- Clamp the virtual reticle to the rounded-rectangle geometry.\n- Avoid abrupt snapping when the reticle reaches the boundary.\n- Recalculate dimensions on viewport resize.\n- Handle different aspect ratios correctly.\n\nThe boundary is a gameplay control region. It does not need to be permanently visible.\n\nIf a visual indicator is useful, use subtle sci-fi HUD corner markings rather than a prominent solid box.\n\nDo not let visual HUD elements interfere with the actual input calculations.\n\n## 3. Preserve the Fixed Pointer Lock Implementation\n\nThe previous physical mouse edge rollback issue has been fixed.\n\nPreserve that successful implementation.\n\nMandatory requirements:\n\n- Continue using Pointer Lock API.\n- Continue using relative mouse movement through movementX and movementY.\n- Do not introduce cursor recentering.\n- Do not depend on clientX, clientY, or absolute cursor coordinates for steering.\n- Do not replace the working input implementation without a demonstrated technical reason.\n- Continue receiving mouse input when the physical mouse reaches the edge of the display.\n- Ensure input is processed exactly once.\n\nBefore modifying this system, inspect the current implementation and identify which parts are responsible for the successful fix.\n\n## 4. Progressive Mouse Steering\n\nPreserve and refine the existing progressive steering behavior.\n\nDesired behavior:\n\n### Zone A \342\200\224 Precision\n\nInside the 45% \303\227 45% rounded-rectangle boundary:\n\n- Normal, precise steering.\n- Stable sensitivity.\n- Predictable mouse-to-aim response.\n- No unnecessary acceleration.\n\n### Zone B \342\200\224 Transition\n\nWhen the virtual reticle reaches the boundary and the player continues moving the mouse outward:\n\n- Steering initially becomes slower.\n- Sensitivity increases progressively with continued outward mouse movement.\n- The transition must be smooth.\n- No sudden sensitivity jump.\n\n### Zone C \342\200\224 Accelerated Turning\n\nWhen the player continues moving the mouse beyond the boundary:\n\n- Ship rotation becomes progressively faster.\n- The player can perform wide and rapid turns.\n- Steering remains predictable.\n- No uncontrollable rotation or sudden spikes.\n- The physical mouse can continue moving indefinitely through relative input.\n\nThe virtual reticle must not escape the configured aiming region.\n\nImportantly, reaching the virtual reticle boundary must never stop the underlying relative mouse input.\n\n## 5. Mathematical Response Curve\n\nInspect the current progressive steering implementation before changing its formula.\n\nKeep the existing implementation if it already produces the intended behavior.\n\nIf refinement is necessary, use a smooth nonlinear response curve.\n\nFor normalized outward displacement e:\n\nf(e) = e^p / (1 + e^p)\n\nThen:\n\nsensitivity(e) = boundarySensitivity + (maxSensitivity - boundarySensitivity) \303\227 f(e)\n\nSuggested starting values:\n\n- Normal sensitivity: 1.0\n- Boundary sensitivity: 0.35\n- Maximum sensitivity multiplier: 2.5\n- Curve exponent: 2.0\n- Smooth recovery time: approximately 0.12 seconds\n\nThese values are starting points. Tune them through actual gameplay testing.\n\nThe transition should be continuous and should not produce visible or perceptible sensitivity jumps.\n\nApply horizontal and vertical steering independently.\n\nAvoid using an arbitrary fixed turn speed that ignores mouse movement.\n\n## 6. Correct Input and Steering Architecture\n\nKeep these responsibilities separate:\n\n1. Pointer Lock input collection.\n2. Relative mouse displacement accumulation.\n3. Virtual reticle positioning and boundary clamping.\n4. Progressive sensitivity calculation.\n5. Ship yaw and pitch control.\n6. Third-person camera follow.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Pointer Lock input is collected once.\n- The virtual reticle is clamped independently from raw mouse displacement.\n- Outward displacement beyond the boundary remains available to the progressive steering calculation.\n- The flight controller owns ship rotation.\n- The camera controller follows the ship.\n- Camera smoothing must not create unnecessary steering latency.\n- Use delta time consistently.\n- Avoid React state updates on every animation frame.\n- Prevent conflicting input or rotation updates.\n\nDo not let the reticle clamp discard the excess mouse movement needed for accelerated turning.\n\n## 7. Flight Feel\n\nThe original Phase 1 flight prototype had a better overall handling feel.\n\nInspect available Git history or previous implementation when possible.\n\nPreserve or restore:\n\n- Responsive yaw and pitch.\n- Smooth rotation.\n- Controlled inertia.\n- Predictable acceleration.\n- Stable third-person chase camera.\n- Existing boost behavior.\n- Consistent handling at different frame rates.\n\nDo not redesign the flight physics.\n\nDo not introduce excessive smoothing to hide input problems.\n\n## 8. Configuration and Settings\n\nKeep the following parameters configurable in a centralized configuration:\n\n- Aim boundary width: 45%.\n- Aim boundary height: 45%.\n- Boundary corner radius.\n- Normal sensitivity.\n- Boundary sensitivity.\n- Maximum sensitivity.\n- Acceleration distance.\n- Response curve exponent.\n- Recovery smoothing.\n\nAvoid scattering these values across unrelated files.\n\nIf the current Settings UI already supports mouse sensitivity, integrate the relevant settings cleanly.\n\nDo not create an unnecessarily complicated settings interface.\n\n## 9. Actual Browser Testing\n\nTest the actual running browser game.\n\nVerify:\n\n1. The aiming region measures 45% of viewport width.\n2. The aiming region measures 45% of viewport height.\n3. The boundary has rounded-rectangle geometry.\n4. The virtual reticle stays inside the boundary.\n5. Normal steering is precise inside the boundary.\n6. Steering becomes slower immediately after reaching the boundary.\n7. Continued outward movement progressively increases turning speed.\n8. Returning inward smoothly restores precision steering.\n9. Mouse input remains continuous at every physical screen edge.\n10. The ship does not stop turning when the virtual reticle reaches its boundary.\n11. Flight handling remains smooth and responsive.\n12. Camera follow and boost behavior remain intact.\n13. Resizing the viewport updates the boundary correctly.\n14. No regression to the previously fixed Pointer Lock behavior.\n\nTest at more than one viewport aspect ratio if possible.\n\nDo not report a test as passed unless it was actually performed.\n\n## 10. Scope Restrictions\n\n- Do not redesign the game.\n- Do not change combat balance, weapons, bot AI, missions, or progression.\n- Do not replace the successful Pointer Lock fix.\n- Do not rewrite unrelated flight systems.\n- Do not introduce unnecessary dependencies.\n- Do not sacrifice existing visual quality.\n- Do not add a hard mouse input limit.\n- Do not allow the virtual reticle to move outside the configured boundary.\n\n## 11. Final Report\n\nAfter implementation, report:\n\n1. Files and systems changed.\n2. Final boundary geometry and dimensions.\n3. How rounded-rectangle clamping works.\n4. How progressive steering uses mouse movement beyond the boundary.\n5. Final sensitivity and acceleration parameters.\n6. Whether original flight handling was preserved or restored.\n7. Actual browser testing results.\n8. Any remaining issues.\n\nThe final result must preserve continuous relative mouse input while providing a bounded, precise aiming region and smooth progressive turning."
    items {
      text: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Final Aim Boundary Configuration & Progressive Steering Refinement\n\nYou are working on STARFALL, a browser-based 3D arcade space combat game.\n\nImplement the final mouse aiming boundary configuration and refine its integration with the existing progressive mouse steering system.\n\nThis is a focused gameplay control task. Preserve the existing working systems.\n\n## 1. Confirmed Design Decisions\n\nImplement these exact settings:\n\n- Aim boundary width: 45% of viewport width.\n- Aim boundary height: 45% of viewport height.\n- Boundary shape: Rounded rectangle.\n- Boundary position: Centered on the game viewport.\n- Steering transition: Smooth progressive acceleration.\n\nThe boundary dimensions refer to the full game viewport, not the browser window or the physical display.\n\nCalculate the boundary using the current game viewport dimensions so it remains correct when the browser is resized.\n\nFor a viewport of width W and height H:\n\n- Horizontal boundary half-extent: 0.225 \303\227 W.\n- Vertical boundary half-extent: 0.225 \303\227 H.\n\nThe virtual reticle should remain inside this rounded-rectangle precision region.\n\n## 2. Rounded Rectangle Boundary\n\nImplement a mathematically consistent rounded-rectangle boundary.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Center the boundary on the viewport.\n- Width = 45% of viewport width.\n- Height = 45% of viewport height.\n- Use configurable corner radius.\n- Clamp the virtual reticle to the rounded-rectangle geometry.\n- Avoid abrupt snapping when the reticle reaches the boundary.\n- Recalculate dimensions on viewport resize.\n- Handle different aspect ratios correctly.\n\nThe boundary is a gameplay control region. It does not need to be permanently visible.\n\nIf a visual indicator is useful, use subtle sci-fi HUD corner markings rather than a prominent solid box.\n\nDo not let visual HUD elements interfere with the actual input calculations.\n\n## 3. Preserve the Fixed Pointer Lock Implementa