e current progressive steering implementation before changing its formula.\n\nKeep the existing implementation if it already produces the intended behavior.\n\nIf refinement is necessary, use a smooth nonlinear response curve.\n\nFor normalized outward displacement e:\n\nf(e) = e^p / (1 + e^p)\n\nThen:\n\nsensitivity(e) = boundarySensitivity + (maxSensitivity - boundarySensitivity) \303\227 f(e)\n\nSuggested starting values:\n\n- Normal sensitivity: 1.0\n- Boundary sensitivity: 0.35\n- Maximum sensitivity multiplier: 2.5\n- Curve exponent: 2.0\n- Smooth recovery time: approximately 0.12 seconds\n\nThese values are starting points. Tune them through actual gameplay testing.\n\nThe transition should be continuous and should not produce visible or perceptible sensitivity jumps.\n\nApply horizontal and vertical steering independently.\n\nAvoid using an arbitrary fixed turn speed that ignores mouse movement.\n\n## 6. Correct Input and Steering Architecture\n\nKeep these responsibilities separate:\n\n1. Pointer Lock input collection.\n2. Relative mouse displacement accumulation.\n3. Virtual reticle positioning and boundary clamping.\n4. Progressive sensitivity calculation.\n5. Ship yaw and pitch control.\n6. Third-person camera follow.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Pointer Lock input is collected once.\n- The virtual reticle is clamped independently from raw mouse displacement.\n- Outward displacement beyond the boundary remains available to the progressive steering calculation.\n- The flight controller owns ship rotation.\n- The camera controller follows the ship.\n- Camera smoothing must not create unnecessary steering latency.\n- Use delta time consistently.\n- Avoid React state updates on every animation frame.\n- Prevent conflicting input or rotation updates.\n\nDo not let the reticle clamp discard the excess mouse movement needed for accelerated turning.\n\n## 7. Flight Feel\n\nThe original Phase 1 flight prototype had a better overall handling feel.\n\nInspect available Git history or previous implementation when possible.\n\nPreserve or restore:\n\n- Responsive yaw and pitch.\n- Smooth rotation.\n- Controlled inertia.\n- Predictable acceleration.\n- Stable third-person chase camera.\n- Existing boost behavior.\n- Consistent handling at different frame rates.\n\nDo not redesign the flight physics.\n\nDo not introduce excessive smoothing to hide input problems.\n\n## 8. Configuration and Settings\n\nKeep the following parameters configurable in a centralized configuration:\n\n- Aim boundary width: 45%.\n- Aim boundary height: 45%.\n- Boundary corner radius.\n- Normal sensitivity.\n- Boundary sensitivity.\n- Maximum sensitivity.\n- Acceleration distance.\n- Response curve exponent.\n- Recovery smoothing.\n\nAvoid scattering these values across unrelated files.\n\nIf the current Settings UI already supports mouse sensitivity, integrate the relevant settings cleanly.\n\nDo not create an unnecessarily complicated settings interface.\n\n## 9. Actual Browser Testing\n\nTest the actual running browser game.\n\nVerify:\n\n1. The aiming region measures 45% of viewport width.\n2. The aiming region measures 45% of viewport height.\n3. The boundary has rounded-rectangle geometry.\n4. The virtual reticle stays inside the boundary.\n5. Normal steering is precise inside the boundary.\n6. Steering becomes slower immediately after reaching the boundary.\n7. Continued outward movement progressively increases turning speed.\n8. Returning inward smoothly restores precision steering.\n9. Mouse input remains continuous at every physical screen edge.\n10. The ship does not stop turning when the virtual reticle reaches its boundary.\n11. Flight handling remains smooth and responsive.\n12. Camera follow and boost behavior remain intact.\n13. Resizing the viewport updates the boundary correctly.\n14. No regression to the previously fixed Pointer Lock behavior.\n\nTest at more than one viewport aspect ratio if possible.\n\nDo not report a test as passed unless 