nt.\n\n\n**Analyzing Independent Steering**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how the independent horizontal and vertical steering interacts with the rounded rectangle boundary, especially after the latest mathematical model for clamping. I am re-examining the critical prompt sections (2, 4, 5, and 6) to ensure the implementation precisely adheres to the specifications. I need to make sure the progressive steering is seamless. I\'m focusing on ensuring smooth, continuous reticle behavior within the boundary, as a primary quality.\n\n\n**Implementing Progressive Steering**\n\nI\'m now implementing the progressive steering mechanics, based on the `f(e) = e^p / (1 + e^p)` function. I\'m focusing on ensuring smooth transitions between normal steering, the slower transition zone, and accelerated turning. I need to make sure the virtual reticle never escapes and the mouse input continues.\n\n\n**Calculating Sensitivity Progression**\n\nI\'m now integrating the sensitivity function. I\'m focusing on ensuring smooth transitions for the reticle\'s sensitivity as it nears and enters the aiming boundary. The implementation involves independently applying progressive sensitivity and maintaining the separation of responsibilities as specified. I\'m testing `sensitivity(e) = boundarySensitivity + (maxSensitivity - boundarySensitivity) \303\227 f(e)` to ensure it smoothly increases the effective sensitivity with the reticle\'s distance from the boundary.\n\n\n**Refining Clamping Logic**\n\nI\'m now refining the clamping logic, paying particular attention to how the \"excess\" mouse movement is handled after clamping. I\'m focusing on ensuring that any \"excess\" beyond the rounded rectangle boundary is preserved and appropriately applied to the accelerated turning mechanics. It\'s becoming clearer how to effectively use the delta between raw and clamped aim to enhance and accelerate steering. I\'m carefully analyzing `deltaX` and `deltaY`, and how they are handled.\n\n\n**Verifying Approach