as the ship returns back into the precision steering zone, and while keeping the ship turning. I\'m carefully analyzing various multiplier values to ensure the behavior is responsive.\n\n\n**Refining Return Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the nuances of returning the reticle to the boundary after accelerated turning. I\'m exploring an \'inwardRetractMultiplier\' that smoothly consumes excess motion when the mouse moves inward. The goal is a responsive transition. I\'m carefully analyzing its impacts on simulated test cases and edge conditions. I\'ve uncovered some potential test cases for boundary overshoots.\n\n\n**Revisiting Limit Adherence**\n\nI\'m now re-evaluating the current implementation, specifically in `FlightController.ts`, within the context of Section 10\'s requirement to avoid hard mouse input limits, and Section 4, that the physical mouse can continue moving indefinitely through relative input. The current clamping of `virtualAimX` contradicts these sections. I\'m focusing on lines 390-403 to modify the clamping behavior, and eliminate the fixed input limit, for a more progressive steering system.\n\n\n**Eliminating Input Limits**\n\nI\'m now focusing on eliminating hard input limits, as the existing clamping of `virtualAimX` contradicts the user\'s requirements. I\'m considering asymptotic accumulation and direct mouse velocity contributions. I\'m exploring the reasoning behind the original clamp and how to prevent unbounded virtual aim while providing limitless mouse input. I\'m evaluating alternative methods that enable continuous movement and predictable turn speed.\n\n\n**Refining Excess Decay**\n\nI\'m now implementing and testing the inward responsiveness. The focus is a smooth excess decay to reduce acceleration as the virtual reticle returns from the boundary. I\'m focusing on ensuring it works well, considering a variable `inwardRetractMultiplier` to ensure a smooth transition back into the precision steering zone.\n\n\n**Refining Reversal Response**\n\nI\'m now implementing the exponential bleed for excess movement upon inward mouse input. The goal is to provide a responsive transition when returning from accelerated turning. I\'m focusing on ensuring excess decays smoothly when the mouse moves inward. The plan is to map the inward reversal proportionally to the current excess. This should prevent sluggishness.\n\n\n**Refining Excess Handling**\n\nI\'m now implementing the asymptotic excess integrator, as an alternative to the previous clamping, ensuring smooth transitions. The \"excessPx\" value will track outward distance, while \"rawAim\" advances with mouse movement. Outward motion will marginally compress excess and add a small live mouse-rate assist. This should provide smooth behavior while avoiding hard input limits.\n\n\n**Evaluating Steering Responsiveness**\n\nI\'m now carefully re-evaluating the current implementation within `FlightController.ts` in light of the user\'s focus on progressive steering and unlimited input. I\'m prioritizing eliminating the clamping of `virtualAimX` to achieve responsive and predictable behavior. I\'m analyzing the interplay between \"excess\" and `sensitivity(e)` to ensure smooth transitions between precision and accelerated steering zones, by mapping the excess mouse motion to a steering force.\n\n\n**Verifying Viewport Coordinate Spaces**\n\nI\'m now carefully tracking viewport dimensions and all their coordinate transforms. I\'m focusing on ensuring data consistency between `FlightCanvas.tsx`, `FlightController`, `FlightHUD`, and `SettingsPanel`. The goal is to avoid discrepancies. I\'ll meticulously verify `viewportWidth` and `viewportHeight` are accurately passed through and used consistently for all calculations, with no coordinate mismatches.\n\n\n**Refining Viewport Consistency**\n\nI\'m now focusing on ensuring consistent viewport dimensions throughout the codebase, particularly between `GameScene.tsx` and `FlightHUD.tsx`. Utilizing `useThree((s) => s.size)` from R3F in `GameScene` to capture exact canvas pixel dimensions, and `ResizeObserver` in `FlightHUD` to measure HUD dimensions, the goal is to avoid discrepancies and coordinate mismatches. I\'m also ensuring that `viewportWidth` and `viewportHeight` are correctly passed through to the `flight.update` method for use in calculations, and I\'m also considering resize-handling mid-flight.\n\n\n**Validating Dynamic Rescaling**\n\nI\'m now implementing the viewport resize behavior. I am ensuring that the `virtualAim` is proportionally rescaled whenever `viewportWidth` or `viewportHeight` changes. Scaling `virtualAimX` by `W / prevW` and `virtualAimY` by `H / prevH`, with immediate clamping, maintains the reticle\'s relative position within the resized boundary. I\'m focusing on ensuring this works seamlessly.\n\n\n**Validating Aspect-Ratio Behavior**\n\nI\'m now verifying the reticle\'s behavior across multiple aspect ratios and resolutions. Specifically, I\'m testing examples across the provided resolutions of 1280x720, 1920x1080, 1024x768, and 1440x600. It\'s clear how the current setup, including `virtualAimX`, accumulates directly in CSS viewport pixels. I\'m focusing on ensuring consistent mouse feel, so no unexpected reticle \"jumps\" occur.\n\n\n**Validating Boundary Sensitivity**\n\nI\'m now confirming the predictable sensitivity changes when traversing the aiming boundary, with the latest mathematical model. I\'ve performed sample calculations and tested on multiple viewport resolutions to ensure smooth and expected transitions. I\'ve re-examined the `accelerationDistance` and the relationship to sensitivity progression, to ensure the direct and intuitive feel.\n\n\n\n**Centralizing Steering Configuration**\n\nI\'m now building the steering configuration file, `/src/config/steering.ts`, to centralize all steering-related parameters, as requested. I\'m focusing on defining `AIM_BOUNDARY_DEFAULTS`, including width, height fractions, and corner radius