s. I\'m confirming that the `clampedX` and `clampedY` calculations accurately project the raw input onto the corner arc. The `excessX` and `excessY` values also precisely reflect the distance beyond the boundary, ensuring correct steering progression.\n\n**Refining Smooth Transitions**\n\nI\'m now meticulously analyzing the smooth blend as the reticle approaches and touches the rounded corner arc. The mathematical model appears robust; the blend from normal sensitivity to boundary sensitivity and the smooth rise of `excessX` at the arc\'s point of contact are mathematically airtight. There are no seams or jumps! I\'m now exploring edge cases. I\'m focusing on validating corner clamp behavior.\n\n\n**Tracing Steering Parameters**\n\nI\'m now carefully tracking how `rawAimX` and `rawAimY` accumulate mouse input, especially near the aiming boundary. I\'m focusing on ensuring responsiveness, including cases with inward mouse input and those with continuous outward movement. I\'m verifying the \"soft excess return\" implementation is smooth, and that it addresses the issue of potentially large `rawAimX` values. I also want to make sure I am fully addressing the user\'s intent.\n\n\n**Testing Inward Deceleration**\n\nI\'m now putting the new inward deceleration model to the test, using specific numbers to validate the responsiveness. The focus is ensuring players can smoothly return to precision steering, especially after deep \"overdrive.\" I\'m also ensuring the math behaves as expected and produces predictable results.\n\n\n**Validating Responsiveness Parameters**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the effect of `inwardGain` and the excess ratio on responsiveness. I\'m confirming that the model effectively unwinds the accumulated excess and returns the reticle to precision steering, especially after significant outward movement, with no hard input limits. The blend is seamless, even with large input values. I\'m validating the test cases for overshoot.\n\n\n**Validating Precision Zone Dynamics**\n\nMy focus is now on the precision steering zone and the boundary\'s edge. I\'m verifying the mathematical handling of inward mouse movements, and it looks like the model is precisely as requested. Zero discontinuity at the boundary! I\'m now exploring how to confirm the transition behavior.\n\n\n**Analyzing Steering Calculation**\n\nI\'m now investigating the calculation of `steerX` and `steerY`, specifically focusing on how clamped coordinates are normalized within the aiming boundary. I am evaluating how normalization handles rounded corners and how their smooth curve influences the `normX` and `normY` values, ensuring the progressive steering correctly uses this deflection. It appears the normalization smoothly reflects the visual deflection on screen.\n\n\n**Validating Steer Progression**\n\nI\'m now carefully analyzing the complete lifecycle of `steerX` progression. After review, it is clear that sensitivity smoothly dips from `1.0` to `0.35` (boundary sensitivity) as the reticle approaches the boundary, resulting in a yaw rate of `0.74 * 2.1 = 1.55 rad/s` at `normX = 0.78`. The rate drops at the boundary. The excess will determine Zone C. I will ensure no visual \"jump\" in steering.\n\n\n**Revising Precision Zone Behavior**\n\nI\'m now carefully re-evaluating the steering progression as the reticle *approaches* the boundary, specifically between `0.80B` and `1.00B`. After review, I\'ve confirmed normal, precise steering with stable sensitivity *inside* the 45% boundary. I will verify that the transition into Zone B correctly modulates sensitivity, and that there is a seamless and continuous transition into the boundary.\n\n\n**Verifying Sensitivity Blending**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the blending behavior within the steering zones and re-evaluating the behavior in Zone B. After revisiting the prompt requirements, I\'m working to verify that sensitivity seamlessly transitions between zones with the correct start and end sensitivities. The behavior must be smooth with no sudden jumps, and no unexpected steering changes. I\'m focusing on validating the `boundaryBlendStart` and related parameters.\n\n\n**Validating Zone B Transitions**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the behavior within Zone B, focusing on the sensitivity blending. The calculations ensure the sensitivity smoothly eases to `0.35` at the boundary without any jumps. I\'m verifying the 0.80 and 0.85 configurations, confirming seamless 1.0x sensitivity within most of the boundary, transitioning to a smooth reduction at the edge, using the `f(e)` function, per request. All blend ratios and calculations are producing the smooth behavior.\n\n\n**Inspecting Steering Responsiveness**\n\nI\'m now diving into the code\'s turn rates and damping. I\'m focusing on responsiveness and inertia. The Interceptor\'s `angularDamping` and `speed` values provide a snappy 63ms response and crisp, controlled 74ms stopping inertia. I\'ll meticulously review the calculations and constants in `FlightController.ts` lines 485-520 to ensure it is as requested.\n\n\n**Examining Turn Rate Limits**\n\nI\'m now carefully analyzing the camera controls, particularly during accelerated turns in Zone C, focusing on the camera\'s lag and rotational tracking. I\'m verifying the interplay between `lagX`, `lagY`, the clamped lag limits and `maxAllowedDist`. I\'m confirming that the camera maintains its position during rapid maneuvers, ensuring it never loses the ship. The goal is to confirm the camera\'s ability to keep up during Zone C, especially at 4.5 rad/s.\n\n\n**Validating Precise Camera Behavior**\n\nI\'m now integrating the camera logic, focusing on maintaining the third-person chase camera feel with zero steering latency, by proportionally tightening camera tracking in Zone C to zero unwanted sluggishness, and ensure all camera parameters function as expected. I will be verifying that camera lag is minimal (e.g., 11\302\260 in a 4.5 rad/s turn).\n\n\n**Validating Input Manager Integrity**\n\nI\'ve just finished a thorough review of `InputManager.ts` and confirmed its complete compatibility with the existing system. The implementation fully aligns with requirements, ensuring clean pointer lock, raw input processing, and robust handling of potential glitches. My priority is seamless integration and I am confident that there is no need to make any changes.\n\n\n**Refining Aiming Boundary Appearance**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the aiming boundary\'s visual representation. I\'m replacing the solid border in `FlightHUD.tsx` with subtle, sci-fi-style corner arc brackets. The goal is a visual cue that doesn\'t obstruct gameplay or interfere with input calculations, ensuring the visual perfectly renders the rounded rectangular region.\n\n\n**Refining Boundary Visuals**\n\nI\'m now implementing the boundary\'s refined visual style. I\'m focusing on the subtle corner arc brackets with tangential ticks, ensuring they have pointer-events-none and minimal opacity, which gently brightens upon approach. I\'ll also expose the boundary\'s live geometry via `data-*` attributes for browser verification, ensuring they correctly reflect the viewport dimensions.\n\n\n**Finalizing Viewport-Relative UI**\n\nI\'ve just refined the viewport integration, ensuring that the reticle\'s screen position and boundary path always update on the next frame after a resize using `clientWidth` and `clientHeight`. This update eliminates layout thrashing. Moreover, I\'m removing the `overdriveReticleStretch` in the `FlightController`, with the reticle\'s position sourced directly from clamped coordinates. Instead, I will animate visual feedback in the `FlightHUD` for the transition zones.\n\n\n**Reviewing Settings Integration**\n\nI\'m now carefully integrating settings into the game. I\'m focusing on creating a new `steering.ts` to centralize configurability, using an existing structure and modifying `ships.ts` and `game.ts` to reflect a more complete configuration. I\'ll meticulously ensure clean integration. I\'ll be defining an `AIM_BOUNDARY_DEFAULTS` and `PROGRESSIVE_STEERING_DEFAULTS` section.\n\n\n**Centralizing Steering Configuration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the steering configuration. I\'m focusing on creating an `AimBoundaryConfig` and `ProgressiveSteeringConfig` interface in `/src/config/steering.ts`, for a single source of truth. I\'ll define `widthFraction`, `heightFraction`, `cornerRadiusRatio`, `normalSensitivity`, and the rest, ensuring seamless configuration.\n\n\n**Defining Steering Parameters**\n\nI\'m now integrating the detailed steering parameters into the configuration, defining `AimBoundaryConfig` and `ProgressiveSteeringConfig` within `/src/config/steering.ts`. I\'m focusing on defining `accelerationDistance`, `responseCurveExponent`, `recoverySmoothingSec`, `deadzone`, `precisionExponent`, `boundaryBlendStart`, and max safety multipliers, with all values defined and tested.\n\n\n**Integrating Steering Configuration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the provided parameters into the configuration file. I\'ll update the `boundarySensitivity`, `maxSensitivity`, and all values into `/src/config/steering.ts`. After a careful review, I\'m integrating the correct default steering config for aim boundary and progressive steering. Finally, I will ensure settings are migrated correctly from local storage, for backward compatibility.\n\n\n**Refining Boundary Defaults**\n\nI\'m now ensuring the aiming boundary\'s default values are correct and consistently applied. I\'ve updated the defaults to 45% x 45% and verified the Settings Panel\'s layout.\n\n\n"
